# Applying to Existing Projects

To apply this template to an existing project, follow these steps:

1. Review the [Prerequisites](prerequisites.md), including [Secrets and Tokens](../reference/token-permissions.md)

1. Make sure your existing project is ready:
    1. It's cloned to your local computer
    1. All changes are committed
    1. Its default branch (usually `main` or `master`) is checked out

1. In the existing project, create a new branch (`copier-template-apply`, for example) from your default branch

1. Clone this repository, open it in your terminal, and install the tools the template needs:

    ```bash
    mise trust
    mise install
    ```

1. From this repository, run the command below, replacing the path at the end with your existing project's root:

    ```bash
    mise x "--" copier copy --trust {% if using_github %}https://github.com/{{ github_repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}{% elif using_azdo %}git+https://dev.azure.com/{{ azdo_org }}/{{ azdo_project_encoded }}/_git/{{ repo_name }}{% endif %} ~/path-to-existing-project
    ```

    See [Template Questions](../reference/template-questions.md) for what each prompt controls.

1. For the Repo Setup Actions question, choose `Set Repo Rules` on GitHub or `None` on Azure DevOps; Azure DevOps reads pipeline YAML from the remote repo, so its pipelines are registered by hand once the files are pushed (see [Manual Repo Settings](../reference/manual-repo-settings.md))

1. Set all other settings as appropriate for your project

1. Once the template is applied, review files that have been changed using git, and stage, discard, or modify as appropriate

1. Commit and push the changes, then merge them via a Pull Request

1. Delete your clone of this repository if you like; the project doesn't need it, since future updates (`mise run copier-update`) fetch the template from its URL

1. You are done, and your project will now operate the same as one that was generated from this template.
